+\subsection{Monte Carlo Scenario Construction}
+EchoForge uses Monte Carlo sampling to vary the public-proxy operating environment while preserving a group-level split. Each scenario group receives a site archetype, range band, aspect bucket, noise/weather regime, hard-negative role when negative, and phase expansion into initial take-up, climb transition, and cruise altitude. The output is a set of locked scenario groups whose phase records inherit a single split key.
+\begin{table*}[t]
+  \caption{Scenario Setup by Flight Phase}
+  \label{tab:scenariophases}
+  \centering\small
+  \begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.18\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}@{}}
+    \toprule
+    Phase & Positive public-proxy stress & Hard-negative stress & Interpretation boundary \\
+    \midrule
+    Initial take-up, 0--30 s & rail/catapult proxy, ground coupling, unsettled Doppler, multipath & birds/RC/ground and clutter may overlap & hardest phase; diagnostic, not field launch model \\
+    Climb transition, 30--90 s & increasing range and more stable speed/aspect & bird/RC overlap, weather cells, multipath ghosts, passive-RF missingness & transition phase; useful for failure slices \\
+    Cruise altitude, 90--150 s & most stable track, cleaner cadence, stronger cross-sensor consistency & high-energy benign movers or RFI & easiest phase in current run; not an operational cruise claim \\
+    \bottomrule
